<p>The MV Hondius cruise ship docked at the Dutch port of Rotterdam on Monday morning for disinfection after a hantavirus outbreak during its voyage killed three passengers and put international health authorities on alert. The vessel arrived with 25 crew members and two medical personnel aboard; passengers had disembarked earlier, and Dutch authorities said the remaining people would enter quarantine. The World Health Organization kept its global public-health risk assessment at &quot;low&quot; but said additional cases could still appear because hantavirus can have an incubation period of several weeks. The outbreak has reached at least 11 cases, nine of them confirmed, according to reports citing WHO figures, and Canada said one of four Canadians in isolation after leaving the ship tested positive Sunday.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cGC4Jv_8x6pNemQbQ4Iv_PvWntYT7IoCGWyU8L09EEwTWLjoD-smA1GleG8q-XI2JXRQjRpa7jMFBiTzL1newD3DDlMbbwE-fQaZOH81WjPxRfRGF5sFC1b8cei3iVfpktpb2z8fjic6wKt0OjNYX_ENo9hb-GWEEKPFidOBAey-gJ7E-VEcCs0Sp9-IPUWplVQfpy06RouXfYUW3vA' alt='The MV Hondius cruise ship is escorted into the Port of Rotterdam, Netherlands, Monday, May 18, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Quarantine site: Dutch broadcaster NOS reported that 23 residential units were prepared at Calandsteiger 7 for foreign crew who could remain there for as long as six weeks if they cannot be repatriated.</li><li>Crew nationalities: The final group included 17 Filipinos, four Dutch nationals, four Ukrainians, one Russian and one Polish citizen, according to Spanish and Dutch accounts of the disembarkation plan.</li><li>No symptoms: Ship operator Oceanwide Expeditions said no one aboard the vessel was experiencing symptoms during the final leg to Rotterdam.</li><li>Quarantine length: WHO has recommended a 42-day quarantine for people at high risk of exposure, while local authorities said it was not yet clear whether all foreign crew would remain for the full period.</li></ul><